{"id":"3f5a1af3-b5d5-40ef-a47f-1236cffa375f","article_key":"doi:10.1016/j.cell.2026.10.031","title":"Toward an integrated framework for a human and mammalian brain cell atlas","title_zh":"迈向人类和哺乳动物脑细胞图谱的整合框架","doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2026.10.031","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2026.10.031","abstract":"The NIH BRAIN Initiative Cell Atlas Network (BICAN) is developing a standardized, integrated reference framework for classifying and studying brain cell types across human, macaque, marmoset, and mouse. We present a cross-species, multimodal atlas of the basal ganglia, a group of subcortical nuclei central to motor control, adaptive behavior, and neurological disease. Built from large-scale single-cell transcriptomic classification and integrated with epigenomic, spatial genomic, multiomic, and developmental datasets, the atlas provides a curated, data-driven taxonomy with standardized nomenclature, extensive phenotypic characterization, and community-informed annotation. Cell types are mapped into species-specific anatomical reference frameworks and linked across species through structural ontologies. Realizing this resource required coordinated standards for tissue acquisition and processing, harmonized donor metadata, anatomical registration, sequencing pipelines, data infrastructure, visualization, mapping tools, and interoperable metadata. The BICAN basal ganglia atlas demonstrates how pipelines and standards can be implemented, validated, and scaled toward a whole-brain human and mammalian cell-type reference.","abstract_zh":"NIH BRAIN 倡议细胞图谱网络（BICAN）正在开发一套标准化的整合参考框架，用于分类和研究人类、猕猴、小 marmoset 和小鼠的脑细胞类型。我们展示了一种跨物种、多模态的基底神经节图谱，这是一组与运动控制、适应性行为和神经系统疾病密切相关的皮层下核团。该图谱构建于大规模单细胞转录组分类之上，并整合了表观基因组、空间基因组、多组学和发育数据集，提供一套经整理的、数据驱动的分类学，配有标准化命名、广泛的表型表征以及社区导向的注释。细胞类型被映射到物种特异的解剖参考框架中，并通过结构本体学在跨物种层面相互关联。实现该资源需要组织获取和处理的协调标准、供体元数据协调、解剖登记、测序管线、数据基础设施、可视化工具、映射工具以及可互操作的元数据。BICAN 基底神经节图谱展示了管线和标准如何被实施、验证和扩展，以构建全脑人类和哺乳动物细胞类型参考。","snippet":"The NIH BRAIN Initiative Cell Atlas Network (BICAN) is developing a standardized, integrated reference framework for classifying and studying brain cell types across human, macaque, marmoset, and mouse. We present a cross-species, multimodal atlas of the basal ganglia, a group of subcortical nuclei central to motor control, adaptive behavior, and neurological disease. Built from large-scale single-cell transcriptomi…","translated_at":"2026-11-14T08:11:26.352478Z","translation_model":"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-super-vl-120b-a12b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":"189","issue":"23","published_at":"2026-11-01T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-11-12T23:34:37Z","display_date":"2026-11-12T23:34:37Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":null,"print_published_at":"2026-11-01T00:00:00","first_author":"Aaron Garcia","authors_text":"Aaron Garcia, Akiko Uematsu, Akira Watanabe, Alan M. Watson, Alexander Monell, Alexander J. Ropelewski, Amber Nolan, Amit Klein, Andrea D. Rivera, Andreas S. Tolias, Anita E. Bandrowski, Anna Bartlett, Antarr Byrd, Anton Kovalsky, Anup Mahurkar, Arnold Kriegstein, Aseel Awdeh, Ashwin Bhandiwad, Austin Macdonald, Benjamin Dichter, Bing Ren, Bing-Xing Huo, Boudewijn Lelieveldt, Brian E. Kalmbach, Brian Herb, Brian Long, Brian R. Lee, Bruce Fischl, Brian Staats, Bryan M. Tran, Burke Q. Rosen, C. Dirk Keene, Cade Reynoldson, Caitlin Latimer, Cameron Bielstein, Camille Lamy, Carol L. Thompson, Cesar Barragan, Chakshu Gandhi, Charles Vanderberg, Chongyuan Luo, Christiaan P.J. de Kock, Christine MacDonald, Christopher Cardenas, Christopher B. Morrison, Chu-Yi Tai, Cindy T. Baez Becerra, Cindy van Velthoven, Cody Baker, Colin Kern, Craig Stark, Dan Yuan, Daniel Child, Daofeng Li, David Davis, David A. Lewis, David C. Van Essen, David Osumi-Sutherland, Delissa McMillen, Derick Aranda, Dorota Jarecka, Dustin Olley, Ed S. Lein, Edwin Monuki, Edyta Vieth, Elizabeth Head, Elizabeth Kiernan, Elysha Fiabane, Emma Osgood, Erge Edgu-Fry, Eric Boone, Erin Schoenbeck, Evan M